/-!
# Exhaustive certificates for the Hermitian four-space comparison

The even permutations have seven orbits on ordered distinct nonzero vectors.
After normalizing the first two columns, 7 × 16² checked cases cover every
binary matrix: a nonzero kernel vector certifies singularity; otherwise two
even permutations express it using the identity, a transposition, or one of
the fourteen original representatives. The original representative words
then produce independent commuting cubics in every forbidden overgroup.
All table and matrix identities are checked by kernel reduction.

A complete ordered-pair family was measured before expansion (47 s including
imports, 2.9 GB peak); the seven families used 3.6 GB. The tables use the
existing low-bit-first convention. They contain certificates, not an assumed
exhaustion or a native-evaluation axiom.
Source: Aschbacher–Smith 8.2.8(1), printed p.719, refs/quasithin.pdf.
-/
